course: dictionary
lesson: 14033
title: A manicure and a manicured lawn
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A manicure is a beauty treatment for your hands and fingernails. Someone cuts, shapes and often paints your nails. «Mum booked a manicure for her birthday, so she sat in the chair while the nail worker shaped her nails.»
explain: Manicure can also be a verb, which means to give someone a manicure: «The nail worker will manicure my nails today.» The word manicured describes something very neat and carefully trimmed. «The park had a manicured lawn with no weeds at all.» Look at the words around it: neat and carefully trimmed tell you what it means.
explain: When you meet a word like manicure, check the context. Ask what word would fit, then try your meaning in the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: A manicure is a beauty treatment for the hands and fingernails.
fact: «Manicure» can be a verb too: to manicure someone's nails means to give them a manicure.
fact: «Manicured» means very neat and carefully trimmed.
fact: People often say have a manicure or get a manicure.
fact: «Manicure and pedicure» is a phrase used for nail treatments, and it shows the word is often used with another nail word.
exercise: «She had a manicure on Saturday, and her nails were painted pink.» What is a manicure? | A type of haircut for the head | A game played with cards | *A beauty treatment for the hands and fingernails | A bath for the feet
exercise: «The gardener manicured the lawn until every blade of grass was the same height.» What does manicured mean here? | Full of weeds | Covered in snow | *Very neat and carefully trimmed | Wet and muddy
exercise: Find the verb in «The nail worker will manicure my nails today.» | today | worker | nails | *manicure
test: «Dad got a manicure while Mum had her hair cut.» What does getting a manicure mean? | *Having your hands and nails cared for | Cutting down a tree | Painting the walls of a room | Walking a long way home
test: Which word describes a garden that is very neat and carefully trimmed? | Muddy | *Manicured | Crowded | Wild
exam: Which sentence uses manicure as a noun? | «The lawn was manicured.» | *«Ella's manicure took an hour.» | «Please manicure the hedge today.»
